        "y0": 1597,
        "x1": 1256,
        "y1": 2065
      },
      "text": "\"Perhaps in thus dramatically fashioning his volume Mr. Villard obeyed an instinct rather than acted upon a preconceived plan; that is often the case with great work, where a writer's feelings are deeply enlisted. Be this as it may, the merit and charm are none the less; he has seized well a splendid opportunity and has written one of the great biographies of our literature.\"\n\nHENRY WATTERSON in the Louisville \"Courier-Journal\"\n\n\"No fault may justly be found with Mr. Villard's telling of the story. It is minute and lucid, altogether fair and unvarnished.\"\n\nFully Illustrated with Portraits, and Other Illustrations\n\nWith Copious Notes and Bibliography $5.00 Net; Postage 20 Cents\n\nBoston HOUGHTON MIFFLIN COMPANY New York\n\nMention THE CRISIS.",
